                                           Judgment on 01.07.2024. 
Sheikh Abdul Awal, J: 
          This criminal appeal at the instance of convict 
appellant Abdul Alim @ A. Alim @ Abdul is directed 
against the impugned judgment and order of conviction and 
Sentence dated 21.06.2012 passed by the learned Joint 
Sessions Judge, 1st Court, Sylhet and Special Tribunal No.7, 
Sylhet in Sessions Case No. 207 of 2008  arising out of  
G.R. No. 60 of 2008 corresponding to Kotwali Police 
Station Case No. 62 dated 22.01.2008 convicting the 
accused appellant under table 1 (Ka) of section 19(1) of 
the Madok Drabbya Niyantron Ain, 1990 and sentencing 

<<PAGE 2>>
 
2
him thereunder to suffer rigorous  imprisonment for a 
period of 5(five) years and to pay a fine of Taka 5,000/-
(five 
thousand) 
in 
default 
to 
suffer 
rigorous 
imprisonment for 01 (one) year more.  
 
At the time of pronouncement of judgment, it 
appears from the record that this Court on 13.11.2018 at 
the time of admission of appeal granted ad-interim bail 
to the accused appellant for a period of 01(one) year, 
which was lastly extended on 23.09.2021 for a period of 
01(one) year and thereafter, no one took any steps to 
extend the order of bail, as a result of which the said bail 
was expired long before on 23.09.2022. In that view of 
the matter, I have no hesitation to hold that the convict 
appellant is fugitive from law and justice. 
 
On a query from the Court Ms. Jobeda Begam, the 
learned advocate appearing for the convict-appellant submits 
that who having failed to make any contact with his client 
and thereby she is not in a position to take any steps to 
extend the order of bail. 
In the case of Anti-Corruption Commission Vs. Dr. 
HBM Iqbal Alamgir, reported in 15 BLC(AD) 44, it has 
been held that the Court would not act in aid of an 
accused person, who is a fugitive from law and justice. 
In view of the above, this Appeal must fail. 

<<PAGE 3>>
 
3
In the result, the appeal is dismissed. Since the 
appeal is dismissed the convict- appellant Abdul Alim @ 
A. Alim @ Abdul is directed to surrender his bail bond 
within 3 (three) months from today to suffer his 
sentence, failing which the Trial Court below shall take 
necessary steps to secure arrest against the convict 
appellant.  
 
Send down the lower Court records at once. 
 
","PETITIONER was sent to prison for five years and fined five thousand taka by a special tribunal in Sylhet for possessing a commercial quantity of narcotics, in a case arising out of a police station case of 2008. He appealed and was enlarged on bail at the admission stage; the bail was extended for the last time in September 2021 and lapsed a year later, and no step was taken afterwards. RESPONDENT is the state. He appeals against the conviction and sentence, contending that they cannot be sustained.","Other: Narcotics Control Act, 1990",REFUSED,"The court noted at the outset of the judgment that the appeal had been listed for hearing with the name of the appellant's advocate on several occasions, that the bail had run out in September 2022 and that no one had taken any step to have it extended. On a query from the court the advocate said she had lost contact with her client and could take no step. In that situation the appellant was held to be a fugitive from law and justice, and the court declined to act in aid of such a person, following the Appellate Division. The appeal failed, with three months given to surrender, failing which the trial court was to secure his arrest.",7740c1f709bf01ee081e862c610db8e759939817d25d07ec95e2b9830989e9fb
