","PETITIONER was convicted by the Sessions Judge at Moulvibazar in 2020 under sections 302 and 34 of the Penal Code and sentenced to imprisonment for life and a fine of fifty thousand taka for the murder of a young man whose body, with a cut on the neck and legs tied to a bamboo pole, was found floating in a canal in July 2011. RESPONDENT is the state. He appeals, contending that the prosecution case rested on a confession that did not implicate him and on a recovery of the victim's mobile phone that was never proved, and that his conviction alone, the other accused having been acquitted, was untenable.",Penal Code 1860,ALLOWED,"The court found the appellant's confessional statement to be lacking in culpability, since he said only that he held one leg at the instance of others and did not implicate himself in any fatal blow, planning or conspiracy, and the trial judge had not relied on the same statement to convict the co-accused who were said to have struck the blows. The recovery of the mobile phone, too, rested on witnesses who knew nothing of its origin and on an officer whose claim was uncorroborated. The court further held that a conviction under sections 302 and 34 could not stand where the appellant alone was convicted. The appeal succeeded and he was acquitted.",f9fccd8bbafcb493743534766c1f7b1d5dbd7f40754f105a67c578e3177e58d8
