Md. Badruzzaman,J 
 
 
 
This Rule was issued calling upon defendant-opposite party No. 
28 to show cause as to why  order dated 26.06.2023  passed by learned 
Joint District Judge, 2nd Court, Narayangonj in Title Suit No. 579 of 2022  
keeping the plaintiff’s  application for interim injunction filed under 
section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure with the records and not 
considering the prayer for ad interim injunction without assigning any 
reason whatsoever should not be set aside and as to why defendant-
opposite party No.28 and any person claiming under him should not be 
restrained by an ad interim order of injunction from evicting the 
plaintiff or its tenants from the suit land. 
 
A Division Bench of this Court while issuing the Rule on 
22.01.2024 directed the parties to maintain status quo in respect of 

<<PAGE 2>>
 
 
 
 
2 
 
possession of the suit land for period of 04(four) weeks which was, 
subsequently, extended time to time  
Facts relevant, for the purpose of disposal of this Rule, are that 
the petitioner, a limited company, as plaintiff instituted Title Suit No. 
579 of 2022 against the opposite parties in 2nd Court of Joint District 
Judge, Narayangonj praying for a decree of declaration of title, 
confirmation of possession and permanent injunction in respect of 
1.6652 acre suit land.  
At the time of filing the suit on 20.09.2022, the plaintiff filed an 
application under Order 39 rule 1 read with section 151 of the Code of 
Civil Procedure for temporary injunction restraining the defendants 
from dispossessing the plaintiff company and its tenants from the suit 
land and the trial Court upon hearing directed the defendants to show 
cause within 30 days from the date receipt of the notice as to why the 
injunction as sought for should not be granted. Defendant No. 1 
appeared in the suit on 20.10.2022. On 26.06.2023 the plaintiff filed an 
application under section 151 of the Code of Civil Procedure praying for 
an order of ad interim injunction restraining defendant opposite party 
No. 28 from entering into the suit property and interfering with the 
peaceful possession of the plaintiff and its tenants of the suit property 
till disposal of the application for temporary injunction. The trial Court 
instead of passing any order on the application kept the same on record 
fixing the next date for filing written objection by the defendant vide 
order dated 26.6.2023. 
Challenging the legality of said order dated 26.06.2023 the 
plaintiff has come up with this application under section 115(1) of the 
Code of Civil Procedure and obtained the instant Rule and order of 
status quo.  

<<PAGE 3>>
 
 
 
 
3 
 
Defendant opposite party No. 28 has entered appearance and 
filed counter-affidavit and supplementary counter-affidavit to contest 
the Rule.  
Mr. Md. Ramzan Ali Sikder, learned Advocate appearing for the 
plaintiff-petitioner submits that temporary injunction matter was 
pending for disposal and being threatened by defendant No. 28, the 
plaintiff filed the application for ad interim injunction against him and 
the matter was to be disposed of on urgent basis but the trial Court 
without considering such aspect, illegally kept the application on 
records and thus, committed an error of law resulting in an error in the 
decision occasioning failure of justice. Learned Advocate further 
submits that at the time of issuance of Rule on 22.01.2024, this Court 
directed the parties to maintain status quo in respect of possession of 
the suit land but opposite party No. 28 neither challenged said order 
before the Appellate Division nor filed application for vacating the order 
and since the substantive applications for injunction have not been 
disposed of as yet, the trial Court should be directed to dispose of the 
applications for injunction on merit by maintaining status  quo till 
disposal of the injunction matter.    
On the other hand Mr. Md. Sharifuddin (Ratan), learned 
Advocate appearing for opposite party No. 28 submits that the plaintiff 
has no prima facie or arguable case to get any order of injunction or 
status quo and accordingly, the trial Court rightly kept the application 
on records and as such, interference is not called for by this Court. 
We have heard the learned Advocates, perused the application, 
the plaint of the title suit, the applications for temporary and ad interim 
injunction, counter-affidavits and other materials available on record. 
On perusing the materials on record, it appears that during pendency of 

<<PAGE 4>>
 
 
 
 
4 
 
the suit the plaintiff filed application for temporary injunction and the 
trial Court directed the defendants to show cause within 30 days by 
order dated 20.09.2022. Admittedly, the application for temporary 
injunction has not been disposed of as yet. It also appears that the 
plaintiff on 26.6.2023 filed an application under section 151 of the Code 
of Civil Procedure against defendant No. 28 praying for restraining him 
from interfering with the peaceful possession of the plaintiff in the suit 
land and the trial Court, upon hearing, without disposing of the 
application kept it on records fixing the next fixed date on 14.11.2023 
for filing written objection. It appears that the trial Court did not 
dispose of the injunction matter on merit and the defendants did not 
file any written statement or written objection in the suit till 
26.06.2023. Defendant-opposite party No. 28 did not also challenge the 
order of status quo passed by this Court before the Appellate Division 
and did not file any application for vacating the order of status quo 
passed by this Court on 22.1.2024.  
In the fitness of things, we are of the view that justice would be 
met if the substantive applications for injunction are disposed of on 
merit by the trial Court within a specified time by maintaining  status 
quo passed by this Court earlier.  
In that view of the matter this Rule is disposed of. 
The learned Joint District Judge, 2nd Court, Narayangonj is 
directed to dispose of the applications for ad interim and temporary 
injunction filed by the plaintiff within 30 (thirty) days from the date of 
receipt of the copy of this order.  
The parties are directed to maintain status quo in respect of 
possession of the suit land till disposal of the applications for injunction. 

<<PAGE 5>>
 
 
 
 
5 
 
Communicate a copy of this judgment to the Court below at 
once.  
 
  
 
 
 
   
 
(Justice Md. Badruzzaman)  
 
 
 
I agree. 
   
         
 
 
              (Mr. Justice Sashanka Shekhar Sarkar) 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
