","PETITIONER is a limited company which sued in Narayanganj for a declaration of title, confirmation of possession and a permanent injunction over about one and two-thirds of an acre, and applied at the outset for a temporary injunction against dispossession. The trial court called on the defendants to show cause but never disposed of that application, and later, when the plaintiff applied for an ad interim order against the twenty-eighth defendant, simply left the application on the record. RESPONDENT No. 28 is that defendant. The company seeks reversal of that order and an ad interim direction restraining eviction.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"The court found that the injunction application had never been decided on the merits, that the defendants had filed no written statement when the second application was moved, and that the twenty-eighth defendant had neither appealed against the status quo ordered by this court nor asked for it to be recalled. In that situation it held that the proper course was not to decide the injunction itself but to require the trial court to do so. It accordingly closed the rule, directed that both the ad interim and the temporary injunction applications be disposed of on merit within thirty days, and kept the status quo until then.",c5703e2e25fdd984cb47339fe12630c036aeebc64fc0ae9f7670f64c0fee8b02
