","PETITIONER is a defendant in a suit in Pabna brought in 1990 for a declaration of title over land that the plaintiff says was held by a woman through sharecroppers and was sold to him by her heirs in 1969 and 1973, the deeds mistakenly naming one khatian instead of another, with the survey record wrongly showing the defendants' predecessors. The trial court threw the suit out and the appellate court decreed it. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs' heirs. The defendants seek restoration of the trial decree, contending that the plaintiff did not derive title from his vendor.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found that the vendor himself, examined as the plaintiffs' first witness, had admitted before the court that he was the rightful owner in possession and had transferred the property to the plaintiff by the two sale deeds, a course the appellate court had rightly accepted, while the trial court's remark that no title passed was contrary to that admission. It further found that the defendants' claim of a permanent tenancy had itself been held by the trial court to be of doubtful value and was not pressed in any appeal. There being no misreading of the evidence, the rule failed and the appellate decree was affirmed.",435ef8798b30224e87641e9c621a56ce095a5f368b0a11eeabf08e72529b9f07
