Helal-ABO     
","PETITIONERs are pre-emptors who brought a pre-emption case in Barisal in 2012 over a deed of gift, lost it by default, had it restored, and finally obtained an ex parte order of pre-emption in 2021. The pre-emptee purchaser then applied for that order to be undone, saying he had no notice, and the revisional court entertained his application and directed a fresh hearing. RESPONDENT No. 1 is the pre-emptee. The pre-emptors seek reversal, contending that the purchaser had been guilty of gross negligence and had deliberately let the case proceed without him.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found that the pre-emptee had indeed been negligent to a serious degree and had taken advantage of irregularities in the trial court, including a failure to notify his advocate when the records came back. It held, however, that the litigation had never been decided on the merits, that it remained undetermined whether the transaction was a gift or a sale in disguise, and that restoring the pre-emptee's application would allow that question to be tried rather than give him the whole relief. Finding no error occasioning a failure of justice, it declined the rule and directed that the application be decided within two months.",c7fb3328092ac45fc328ddca492c1667b1b07f579bea83b151996379ca7cb999
