","PETITIONER is a plaintiff who sued in Dhaka for specific performance and other reliefs and obtained an ex parte decree in 2022 when the sole defendant did not appear. The defendant then applied for that decree to be undone, saying he learned of it only in April 2023 through a representative, and the trial court acceded to his application. RESPONDENT is that defendant. The plaintiff seeks restoration of the ex parte decree, contending that summons had been served and, in the alternative, that the trial court's order was passed without any discussion of the evidence of either side.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"The court examined the record and found that the trial judge had not touched at all upon the evidence led by either party on the central question whether summons had been served on the defendant, although the onus lay on the plaintiff to meet the assertion of non-service by producing the process server. It also found that the defendant had failed to produce the person through whom he claimed to have learned of the decree, and instead examined an attorney who knew nothing of it. Since the order had been passed in a slipshod manner, the court closed the rule and remanded the application for a fresh decision on the evidence, staying the suit meanwhile.",ca404b69e1c7c2ac0df407ffbc049c0628ae94e89c2c4d5bd9e00b68dc3b086b
