Kawsar /A.B.O 
","PETITIONER is a borrower who sued a nationalised bank in Khulna for over nineteen crore taka, saying the bank had stored pledged raw jute badly and then sold it at a shockingly low price, causing him heavy loss. After the bank encashed fixed deposit receipts lodged as security and adjusted the proceeds against his loan, he applied for them to be restored to their former position, and the trial court turned that application down along with his request that the defence written statement be shut out. RESPONDENT No. 1 is the bank. He seeks reversal of that order.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court applied the same reasoning as in the companion revision: no order had restrained encashment at the material time, the bank acted on the security and applied the proceeds to the loan, and a receipt that has been encashed cannot be put back as it was. It found that the trial judge had adequately cautioned the bank and had rightly taken the written statement on record, since the defendants had filed it on their second appearance. Finding no impropriety or illegality in the order, it declined the rule and directed the suit to be disposed of within six months.",8ae4edf7e4351044f270c86795e84e7a502fd86c7bde5512ea283a8409893486
