","PETITIONERs are a shipping company and others, defendants in a suit in Chattogram in which the plaintiffs, who are neither shareholders nor directors, seek to have a clause in the company's memorandum declared forged, together with the company's stevedoring licence and ship-handling enlistment declared void and an injunction against its operations. Their application for rejection of the plaint was turned down at trial and in revision. RESPONDENT No. 1 is a plaintiff. The defendants seek the plaint to be thrown out, contending that the company court alone has jurisdiction over such a challenge.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court read sections 12 to 16 of the Companies Act together and found that the company court has no power to grant the reliefs sought, since the machinery for altering a memorandum is open only to members, whereas the plaintiffs are strangers to the company. It held that the jurisdiction of the civil court was therefore not ousted, no statute having expressly barred it, and that the plaintiffs, alleging that they are adversely affected by a forged clause, could bring their claim before that court. Since fraud was alleged, the question was one of mixed law and fact requiring evidence. Leave was declined.",a2683aabb0948791285b8fa553eacf44dc93bba2b7d302347f119b15ca575909
