","PETITIONERs are two directors of a power company who were named as judgment debtors in a money execution case in Dhaka, and who applied to have their names struck out on the ground that they hold no shares in the company and were merely nominees of a shareholder. The trial court turned that application down and they came to this court. RESPONDENT is the decree holder. They seek their removal from the execution case, contending that directors of a company are neither necessary nor proper parties to a suit against the company unless there is a specific allegation of misappropriation against them.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,ALLOWED,"The court reviewed the record and the law on the position of directors and held that a director owes no fiduciary or contractual duty to those who deal with the company, that the liability of directors is confined to cases of fraudulent trading or misfeasance, and that a party dealing with a company cannot make its directors personally liable for its breaches of contract by suing them for mismanagement. In the absence of any allegation of that kind, the petitioners were neither necessary nor proper parties to the execution. The rule succeeded, their names were struck out, and the execution case was directed to be finished within four months.",1f485d6c9c695daf5d47a7df48ab270a11f603f2131549d138778a664651da46
