","PETITIONERs are defendants in a partition suit begun in 1961 in Kishoreganj, which has passed through a preliminary decree, several commissioners' reports and a number of revisions. Their latest grievance is that a revisional court directed the plaintiffs to deposit six thousand taka as commissioner's fee for a fresh survey of their share, a step they say is unnecessary because an earlier commissioner's report is still in force. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs' heirs. The defendants seek reversal of that direction, contending that no fresh commission was needed and that the report already on record should be acted upon.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court found that the report of the commissioner appointed in 1985 had been restored and was still in force, so that appointing a new commissioner to survey the same share was redundant, and that the revisional order was based on sound reasoning. It noted that transfers had taken place during the pendency of the suit and that the transferees would stand in the shoes of the plaintiffs. Observing that the suit had been pending for more than sixty-three years, it declined the rule but directed the trial court to make the preliminary decree final within three months without fail.",1fe51af73ae569314f82330008487a59f86b59f3e78b7a37feba8d33f3ac693b
