","PETITIONER is a judgment debtor in a small cause execution case in Habiganj, who applied for the execution to be abated because the decree holder died while it was pending. The heirs of the decree holder resisted the application, and the small cause court turned it down, holding that abatement does not apply to execution proceedings and that the legal representative of the dead decree holder can be brought on record. RESPONDENT is the decree holder's side. The judgment debtor seeks reversal, contending that the execution must be treated as abated.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court examined the statutory provisions on abatement and found that the trial court, which is also the small cause court, had correctly held that a decree does not abate by reason of a party's death after the hearing has concluded and before judgment, and that the special rule on death applies to suits and not to execution. It further found that the door remained open to bringing the dead decree holder's legal representative on record, and observed that the application was in substance a device to prolong the execution so that the fruits of the decree could not be enjoyed. The rule failed and the execution was to be finished within six months.",668df1631d18f4e36b39500c62c3ed8d504728b5a619fa40adb189c6920e4c0b
