","PETITIONER is the mother of a boy of about ten, whose former husband sued in the Family Court at Dhaka for custody and obtained a decree, confirmed on appeal. She had sued for custody herself and says the father had treated her with cruelty and that the child wishes to remain with her. RESPONDENT is the father. She seeks reversal of the concurrent decrees, contending that the welfare of the child, who is doing well at school but is attached to his mother, required that he be left with her.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,PARTLY,"The court examined the evidence and the pleadings of both parents and weighed their social, financial and educational circumstances, and found that the welfare and best interest of the boy would be better protected in the custody of his father, since a Muslim father is the natural guardian and the mother's right of custody is exercised under his supervision. It therefore declined to interfere with the concurrent findings on custody. The court held, however, that the mother's right of access could not be ignored, and it recast the visiting arrangement so that the boy spends time with her on fixed days each week until he attains majority.",9c5b8ee8077267183e9863c4040ca7111aa409e0302e2eb1cd9989811abd08ea
