","PETITIONERs are plaintiffs who sued in Naogaon for a perpetual injunction to stop the head of a school from appointing three persons to the posts of office assistant, night guard and attendant. The defendants applied for the plaint to be thrown out, saying the plaintiffs had ceased to be members of the managing committee and had no standing, and that the suit was barred by law. The trial court declined to throw it out for want of evidence, and the revisional court reversed that and threw it out. RESPONDENTs are the defendants. The plaintiffs seek restoration of the trial court's order.","Other: Intermediate and Secondary Education Ordinance, 1961",REFUSED,"The court found that the revisional court had correctly held that the plaintiffs had no personal interest in the subject matter of the suit and so lacked standing to bring it, and that the suit was in any event not maintainable in view of the statutory bar on a bare injunction, and was further barred by the provision of the education ordinance ousting the civil court from the internal management of a statutory body. It observed that the result of the suit was plain and that a stillborn plaint should be buried at the outset rather than tried. The rule failed and the order throwing out the plaint stood.",8919b9b68ed66594a2d58e2bbdbfbc07eea7e7f88992b756228f5f8afba192f4
