","PETITIONER was convicted by an Additional Metropolitan Sessions Judge in Dhaka in 2016 under section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act and sentenced to three months' simple imprisonment and a fine of one and a half lakh taka over a cheque given for a loan which was dishonoured for want of funds, the statutory notice having been ignored. RESPONDENTs are the state and the complainant. He appeals against the conviction and sentence, contending that the offence was not proved, that the cheque was not given against a subsisting debt, and that the statutory notice was defective.","Other: Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881",REFUSED,"The court noted that nobody appeared for the appellant and that the bail conferred at admission in 2018, last extended in 2019 for a year, had run out in 2020 with no step taken to extend it, so that he was a fugitive from law and justice in whose aid the court would not act. On the merits it found that every element of the offence was present, that the trial judge had considered the material aspects and reached a just conclusion, and that no ground existed to disturb the conviction. The appeal failed on both counts, and the complainant was permitted to withdraw half the amount deposited.",2577134c709cc1eb9b51f1ee6502361e5470a0b2feb741a86bb2fa5fcfa87252
