sentence in accordance with law, failing which the trial 
Court concerned shall take necessary steps against the 
convict-appellant,  Md. Borkat Ali Biswas to secure arrest 
against him. 
Send down the lower Court records at once.   
","PETITIONER was sent to prison for three years and fined five thousand taka under the narcotics statute, after a police team acting on a tip stopped a motorcycle near a river ghat in Faridpur and recovered forty-six bottles of phensedyl, the rider having no papers for them. RESPONDENT is the state. He appeals, contending that the conviction cannot be sustained on the evidence, that the bottles were not recovered from his possession, and that the prosecution witnesses were not independent of the police party. He says he was merely passing along the road when he was stopped.","Other: Narcotics Control Act, 1990",PARTLY,"The court examined the depositions and found that the witnesses had spoken consistently about the time, place and manner of the recovery, corroborating each other, and that the appellant had failed to show any document to justify his possession of the bottles, so the conviction stood. Considering, however, that he had suffered the strain and harassment of a long prosecution and had served part of his sentence, it maintained the fine but reduced the substantive term from three years to two. The appeal therefore failed as to conviction and succeeded only on the question of sentence.",37245b6cae12e7467a3a7627988e6a8274862e38154c61b6a1b29d9963215895
