","PETITIONER was convicted by an Additional Sessions Judge at Chapainawabganj in 2019 under section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act and sentenced to a year's imprisonment and a fine of sixteen lakh taka, over a cheque for fifteen lakh twenty-three thousand three hundred and eighty-seven taka drawn on an Islami Bank branch which was dishonoured for want of funds. A legal notice was sent and ignored, and the complaint was filed within time. RESPONDENTs are the state and the payee. He appeals against the conviction and sentence, contending that the offence was not proved, though nobody appeared for him at the hearing.","Other: Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881",REFUSED,"The court examined the complainant's evidence and the exhibits and found that the cheque, the dishonour slip, the legal notice with its postal receipt and the complaint itself had all been proved, that the notice had been sent within the statutory period and the case filed in time, and that every element of the offence was present. It found no flaw in the reasoning of the trial judge and no ground on which the conviction could be disturbed. The appeal therefore failed, the conviction and sentence were affirmed, the bail and the stay of realisation of the fine were recalled, and the amount deposited at the time of filing was made over to the complainant.",a1391613c4892a9b3f4cfe428333ce8d8285f90afc5641e0491075a288615a07
