","PETITIONER was convicted by an Additional Metropolitan Sessions Judge at Dhaka in 2019 under section 138 of the Negotiable Instruments Act and sentenced to four months' simple imprisonment and a fine of twenty lakh taka, over a cheque for twenty lakh taka issued to a company, dishonoured for want of funds, with the statutory notice going unheeded. RESPONDENTs are the state and the company. During the hearing of his appeal the parties reported a settlement, the amount claimed having been paid, and they filed a joint compromise application asking that the appeal be acceded to and the conviction effaced.","Other: Negotiable Instruments Act, 1881",ALLOWED,"The court read the revisional application together with the compromise application, the impugned judgment and the submissions of both sides, and found it established that an amicable settlement had been reached between the parties and that neither had any claim left against the other. It noted that the object of such a proceeding is recovery of the amount, which had been paid and settled. On those facts it held that the conviction could not be maintained and that interference was warranted. The appeal was acceded to on the basis of the compromise, the conviction and sentence were vacated, and the appellant was acquitted.",4a6cd6df65d20942d4fb26d338e3acadfc726f867a1bbbc41a4d34a6338ab816
