","PETITIONERs are the successors of a purchaser who bought land in Dhaka through a decree for specific performance passed in 1963 and a sale deed registered in 1968, and who sued for a permanent injunction when the authorities threatened to disturb their possession. Their suit was decreed, and the appellate court acceded to the government's revisional application and abated the proceeding under section 13 of the statute on transfer of vested property, on the footing that the land stood published in the list of vested property. RESPONDENTs are the deputy commissioner and the state. The petitioners seek restoration of their case.","Other: Arpito Sampotti Prottarpon Ain, 2001",REFUSED,"The court examined the statutory provision and found that once the land has been published in the list of vested property, any proceeding in a civil court concerning it must abate, the only forum being the tribunal constituted under the statute, where a case filed by the petitioners was already pending. It held that the petitioners' remedy lay there and nowhere else, and that the appellate court had committed no error of law occasioning a failure of justice in abating their proceeding. The rule was therefore closed with those observations, the interim stay was recalled, and no order as to costs was passed.",b430fa3dfffe3ee7417a05c7632655819f004457f5835906afabf520a80dbc03
