and 11 respectively). However, for rendering their respective services 
they are entitled to receive “cÖ‡bv`bv ” “incentive” on the tax so has been 
paid by the assessee under Sections 173 and 178 on his declared income 
in the ratio as provided under Rule 12. In other words, no separate charge 
is payable by the assessee for preparing and furnishing tax return. 
However, his certificate is liable to be cancelled for violation of the 
contexts as prescribed under Rule 17. 
Thus, it is apparent that (a) vide Rules, 2023 the Board has 
introduced a new class of persons upon including Tax Lawyers [Rule 
4(2)] being enlisted and certified by NBR with Unique Identification 
Number (Rule 5) whose sole duties and responsibilities is to prepare and 
furnish tax return on behalf of the respective new as well as old assessee 
only through online platform;  
(b) For rendering their respective services to the assessee they 
cannot charge fees from the respective tax payer but will receive “cÖ‡bv`bv 
” at the respective ratio on the tax to be deposited basing on the income 
of the said tax payer. However, engaging a TRP is the absolute discretion 
of the respective assessee.  
(c) A TRP cannot be engaged as an Authorized Representative of 
an assessee to appear on his behalf in connection with the proceedings 
under the Ain, 2023 before the concerned tax Authority or the Tribunal 
concerned; 

<<PAGE 19>>
 
19
(d) A TRP is not authorized to prepare and furnish revise return on 
behalf of the assessee;  
(e) A TRP shall not be considered as a “Ll BCeS£h£” under Section 
327; and 
(f) With the introduction of this new class without affecting the 
rights and interests of the “Authorized Representative” as defined in 
Section 327 of the Ain, 2023 scope of employment has been created in 
addition to expansion of tax net for lawful collection of tax from the 
respective tax payers.   
In view of the above, it can clearly be discerned that there is a clear 
line of demarcation between the status and functions of an Authorized 
Representative (AR) and a Tax Return Preparer (TRP) and that 
considering the context of the Ain, 2023 and the Rules, 2023 the creation 
of this new class cannot be said to have offended the fundamental rights 
of the “Ll BCeS£h£” as guaranteed under Article 40 read with Article 31 of 
the Constitution. 
Moreso, the Rules, 2023 having been framed by the NBR under 
Section 343 of the Ain, 2023 it is a law in view of Article 152 of the 
Constitution. However, the petitioners have miserably failed to show how 
and to what extend the impugned Rules, 2023 are inconsistent with the 
Constitution and or the parent Act, 2023. 
Considering the above, we have no manner of doubt to find that 
neither the petitioners Associations nor its respective members are 
aggrieved persons within the meaning of Article 102 of the Constitution.  
Last but not the least, the petitioners Associations have filed the 
instant writ petition in a representative capacity espousing the personal 

<<PAGE 20>>
 
20
cause of their respective members, not a public cause involving public 
wrong or public injury.  
 
In view of the above and considering the facts and circumstances of 
the instant case it is found by this Court that the petitioners Associations 
have no locus standi to maintain the instant writ petition. Accordingly, we 
find it redundant to make observations on the other issues being raised by 
the petitioners Associations.  
 
In the result, this Rule is discharged without any order as to costs. 
 
The order of stay granted earlier by this Court is hereby vacated.  
Communicate the judgment and order to the respondents concerned 
at once. 
 
Muhammad Mahbub Ul Islam, J: 
 
 
I agree.    
 
 
Montu (B.O) 
 
 
","PETITIONERs are two associations of tax lawyers, registered societies, who challenged the rules of 2023 framed by the revenue board under the income tax statute creating a new class of tax return preparers, and the advertisement inviting applications for enlistment as such. They say the rules travel beyond the parent statute and offend the constitutional guarantee of freedom of profession, and that the new class encroaches on the work of authorised representatives. RESPONDENTs are the revenue board and the government. The petitioners, suing in a representative capacity, seek a declaration that the rules and the advertisement are void.","Other: Income Tax Act, 2023",REFUSED,"The court compared the functions of an authorised representative under the statute with those of a tax return preparer under the rules and found a clear line of demarcation between the two, the new class being confined to first-time assessees and barred from revising returns or acting before the appellate authorities. It held that creating that class did not offend the constitutional guarantee of profession, that the rules are law within the meaning of the Constitution, and that the petitioners had not shown how the rules conflict with the parent statute. It further found that the associations and their members were not persons aggrieved and had no standing to sue in a representative capacity for a private cause.",9d5c8e06180186a0e695de861d5873779b06fc23d1bce24e7aaeb687fa1749ed
