Upon meticulous examination of the provision of Order XI, 
rule 1 of the Code of Civil Procedure, it appears that a proviso is 
appended in rule 1 providing that the interrogatories which do not 
relate to any matter in question in the suit shall be deemed to be 
irrelevant, notwithstanding that those might be admissible in the 
oral cross-examination of a witness. Meaning thereby, the 
interrogatories may not be relevant or beyond the relevancy of 
plaint within the scope of Order XI rule 1 of the Code of Civil 
Procedure, however, those questions may be brought into the oral 
cross-examination of a witness.  

<<PAGE 8>>
8 
 
From the aforementioned provision, it appears to this Court 
that although the defendant has ample opportunity to raise those 
questions in the oral cross-examination of plaintiffs‟ witnesses but 
the same cannot be allowed to be delivered as written 
interrogatories under Order XI, rule 1 of the Code of Civil 
Procedure, 1908. 
 
In the premise above, this Court does not find any merit in 
the Rule. 
 
Accordingly, the Rule is discharged. 
 
No order as to cost. 
 
The order of stay granted at the time of issuance of the Rule 
is hereby recalled and vacated. 
  
 
 
 
 
 
