","PETITIONERS are defendants in a suit for a mandatory injunction concerning property dedicated to two deities, in which the plaintiffs, as successors of the serving shebayet, seek distribution of the surplus usufruct of the endowment among them after meeting the cost of worship. The first defendant applied for leave to deliver written interrogatories under Order XI of the Code, which the trial court declined and the revisional court affirmed. RESPONDENTs are the plaintiffs. The defendants seek reversal of those orders, contending that the questions, though outside the plaint, could still be put in writing.",Code of Civil Procedure 1908,REFUSED,"The court examined the rule on interrogatories and its proviso, which deems irrelevant any question that does not relate to a matter in issue in the suit, even if it might be admissible in oral cross-examination. It found that the questions proposed went well beyond the plaint, questioning the extent of the endowment and the entitlements of the shebayet's successors, matters not in issue in a suit confined to distribution of surplus usufruct. It held that the defendants retain the liberty to put those questions to the plaintiffs' witnesses in cross-examination, but they cannot be delivered as written interrogatories. The rule therefore failed.",b9021cd123d8b2f3987c1159bcd8a405a1abf10a1e9882f60d4a64510793fb66
