Admittedly,  Jaycee Fashions, of which the petitioner 
was the managing director, held 5% shares of the respondent 
No. 6 company which is the defaulter borrower. Therefore, as 
per explanation to Section 5(NN), Jaycee Fashions cannot be 
treated as interested establishment (ü¡bÑ pw¢nÔø fÐ¢aù¡e) within the 
meaning of Section 5(NN). Hence, Jaycee Fashions cannot be 
termed as defaulter borrower as being ü¡bÑ pw¢nÔø fÐ¢aù¡e  as per 
definition of defaulter borrower given in Section 5(NN). 
Moreover, the petitioner had already resigned from the board of 
directors of Jaycee Fashions which was approved by the 

<<PAGE 5>>
 
Page # 5
concerned bank as per requirement of Section 27L of the Bank 
Companies Act, 1991.  
Subsection (5) of Section 27M provides that the aggrieved 
person or establishment identified as wilful defaulter under 
subsection (4) may prefer an appeal to Bangladesh Bank and 
the decision of Bangladesh shall be final. In the case in hand, 
the petitioner was not treated as wilful defaulter and 
accordingly, subsection (5) providing an appellate forum does 
not apply to the case.   
In view of the foregoing discussions, both on facts and 
law, we find merit in the Rule.  
In the result the Rule is made absolute. The concerned 
respondents are directed to strike out the name of the petitioner 
from the CIB report as defaulter borrower provided the 
petitioner is not a defaulter borrower or wilful borrower in 
accordance with law for any other accounts other than that of 
the respondent No. 6 Sinha Dyeing and Finishing Ltd.  
 
Sardar Md. Rashed Jahangir, J. 
 
 
 
 
 
 
 
I agree. 
 
Kabir,BO. 
","PETITIONER was a shareholder and managing director of a private company holding five per cent of the shares of a dyeing and finishing company which defaulted on bank loans, and his name was published in the central bank's credit information report as a defaulter in connection with that company's borrowings. He had transferred his entire shareholding and resigned years earlier, with the lender's consent. RESPONDENTs are the central bank, the lending bank and the borrowing company. He seeks a declaration that the publication of his name is without lawful authority and a direction that his representation for removal of his name be decided.","Other: Bank Companies Act, 1991",ALLOWED,"The court examined the statutory definition of a defaulter and the explanation to it, which fixes a threshold of twenty per cent shareholding before a related establishment can be treated as an interested establishment, and found that the petitioner's former company held only five per cent and so stood outside that definition. It also found that he had resigned from that company's board with the bank's approval and that his representation had been left undecided, the bank merely sitting silent on it. The rule therefore succeeded, and the authorities were directed to strike his name out of the report, subject to his not being a defaulter on any other account.",922c003f4d165357c2479d17bb5ca34c4f31bc9ca52cccd564e6180addac7acd
